TESS light curves
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
js/data/observations/**
Derived from light curves observed by NASA's Transiting Exoplanet Survey
Satellite (TESS) and served by the Mikulski Archive for Space Telescopes
(MAST) at the Space Telescope Science Institute: https://archive.stsci.edu/

NASA mission data are released without restriction on reuse, so no license
comes with these light curves and Gravitas adds none. What is asked for is an
acknowledgment of the mission and the archive, in the words the mission
suggests, and a citation of the data:

  This work includes data collected by the TESS mission, which are publicly
  available from the Mikulski Archive for Space Telescopes (MAST). Funding
  for the TESS mission is provided by NASA's Science Mission Directorate.

  TESS Light Curves - All Sectors, STScI/MAST, doi:10.17909/t9-nmc8-f686

  Ricker et al. (2015), JATIS 1, 014003, doi:10.1117/1.JATIS.1.1.014003 - the
  mission. Jenkins et al. (2016), Proc. SPIE 9913, 99133E,
  doi:10.1117/12.2233418 - the SPOC pipeline that made the light curves.

The bundled light curve of HD 209458 (TIC 420814525, sector 56) keeps the
cadences SPOC flagged as good, divides by the median flux and averages into
20-minute bins, stored as 16-bit integers in parts per million. Each step,
the archive URL and SHA-256 checksums of the file MAST served and of the
module as committed are recorded in data-packs/tess-hd209458-s56-lc.json.
`npm run packs:provenance` rebuilds the module from the cached file and
compares byte for byte.

